            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "Leah"
            },
            "reviewBody": "This was delicious! The pomegranate molasses added another dimension to an otherwise traditional BBQ sauce! I added more fresh ground black pepper as a preference and used agave nectar instead of honey, and EVOO instead of sunflower oil."
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "3"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "FunkyPear1503"
            },
            "reviewBody": "Hang back off the salt until you taste it. I followed the recipe and it's too much for my taste. You know what they say, you can put more in, but you can't take it out. Also, of you don't want that rare garlic flavour you can cook it out for a minute in a saucepan. Being to a bare simmer and turn it off to cool. The tang is very tasty from the pomegranate though..",
            "datePublished": "2025-05-31T04:34:55.679Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "4"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "JollyMeal1167"
            },
            "reviewBody": "Sounds good 👍 want to make this one",
            "datePublished": "2024-10-28T01:24:51.925Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "5"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "GoldGin6448"
            },
            "reviewBody": "I made this for tonkatsu. Didn't put in salt (soy sauce neither kosher) because I prepared the meat on Sunday but only used it on Thursday. Neither did I use black pepper because I'm not a fan, but my ketchup had a little spice in it. I also pressed garlic instead of slicing, but it's neither here nor there. I forgot to salt the pork before breading too, and salted the breading in the end 😅. The marinade greatly enhanced the flavour profile, giving tonkatsu kind of subtle fruity notes that pair well with pork and breading. I'm a new fan of narsharab, and this recipe is a keeper.",
            "datePublished": "2024-10-11T17:51:41.350Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "5"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "Tracy Gould"
            },
            "reviewBody": "Made this for pulled pork I had left over. This is delicious! The pomegranate molasses gives it a sweet tart kick that is delightful.",
            "datePublished": "2019-07-22T23:12:23.447Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "5"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "Terri Watts Graber"
            },
            "reviewBody": "I'll cut back on the honey next time but it was delicious! I used EVOO instead of Sunflower Seed Oil because seriously, who stocks that? We dipped kettle chips in it and yum......",
            "datePublished": "2019-04-08T11:21:32.873Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "5"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "Buckwheat Queen"
            },
            "reviewBody": "This is quite delicious and super quick to throw together. It’s tangy and not too sweet. Thanks for the recipe.",
            "datePublished": "2019-02-14T16:55:30.430Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "5"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "SassyCook"
            },
            "reviewBody": "I used regular molasses and canola oil for this, only because that's what was around. LOVE it! We're an incredibly busy family (who isn't?) and it saves time to prep ahead. I've made this in a larger batch and stored it in the kitchen for a week. We've used it on roasts, burgers, &amp; chicken. The roast went in the slow cooker with this. For the burgers, I had made sliders and left a little out as one of the sauce choices. For the chicken, we had breaded cutlets and I drizzled some over them. It's been a versatile sauce. Good stuff, thanks for posting it!",
            "datePublished": "2013-10-17T09:40:47.840Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "5"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "Lesley Carol"
            },
            "reviewBody": "Inspired combination. It's now a house favourite. This sauce is 'ace' on pork chops or pork tenderloin, roasted in the oven. As Axel Steenberg warns, this sauce can burn after 30-45 minutes so watch closely. I'm a great fan of pomegranate molasses. I picked it up 'on spec' at the neighbourhood Middle Eastern grocery.",
            "datePublished": "2012-02-15T09:14:44.620Z"
          }
